# School Operating System — environment configuration
#
# Copy to `.env` and edit. `.env` is gitignored; this file is committed, so it
# must never contain a real credential.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Every variable below is genuinely read by the application. The file it is
# read in is named for each one, so this list can be checked against the code
# (tests/env-example.test.ts does exactly that, in both directions).
#
# Sections:
#   1. Required in production
#   2. Database TLS
#   3. Deployment-specific (only needed in some topologies)
#   4. Optional tuning
#   5. Development only
#   6. Not set here (documented so their absence is not a mystery)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Must be exactly `production` for a real deployment. This is not cosmetic; it
# switches on several protections at once:
#   - DATABASE_URL becomes mandatory (see below)
#   - session cookies get the `Secure` flag  (src/lib/auth/session.ts)
#   - the demo credentials panel is hidden   (src/app/login/LoginForm.tsx)
#   - the ephemeral-storage warning is armed (src/lib/operations/storageConfig.ts)
#   - the sandbox origin wildcard is dropped (src/lib/config/origins.mjs)
# Read in: src/db/config.ts and the files above.
NODE_ENV=production

# PostgreSQL connection string. REQUIRED when NODE_ENV=production — the app
# refuses to boot without it rather than silently falling back to the embedded
# development database and writing a school's data somewhere it will be lost.
# The protocol must be `postgres:` or `postgresql:`.
# Read in: src/db/config.ts, src/db/client.ts, scripts/migrate.ts
#
# PostgreSQL 15 or newer is required. Migration 0010 uses NULLS NOT DISTINCT,
# and `npm run db:migrate` refuses to run on anything older.
#
# EXAMPLE ONLY — replace the host, database and credentials, and never commit
# the real value:
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://school_os:CHANGE_ME@db.example.internal:5432/school_os


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. DATABASE TLS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# How to treat the database connection's TLS certificate.
# Read in: src/db/config.ts (resolveSslConfig)
#
#   verify     (default) require TLS and verify the server certificate
#   no-verify  require TLS but accept a self-signed certificate
#   false      disable TLS entirely
#   disable    same as `false`
#
# Leaving this unset means `verify`, which is the safe default. An unrecognised
# value is a hard startup failure rather than a silent downgrade, so a typo
# such as `PG_SSL=tru` cannot quietly disable encryption.
#
# Note: if DATABASE_URL already carries `?sslmode=...` and PG_SSL is unset, the
# URL wins and is passed through untouched.
#
# Use `false` only for a Unix socket or a genuinely trusted private network.
# PG_SSL=verify

# PEM-encoded CA certificate, for a provider whose root is not publicly
# trusted. Preferred over PG_SSL=no-verify, which disables verification.
# Read in: src/db/config.ts
#
# This is a certificate, not a secret, but it is long; most deployments supply
# it as a multi-line value or a mounted file read into the environment.
# A wrong or missing root shows up as SELF_SIGNED_CERT_IN_CHAIN or
# UNABLE_TO_VERIFY_LEAF_SIGNATURE at startup.
# PG_CA_CERT="-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----
# ...
# -----END CERTIFICATE-----"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. DEPLOYMENT-SPECIFIC
#    Correct values depend on how the app is hosted. Wrong values are worse
#    than unset, so each default is the safe one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where uploaded documents are stored. Defaults to `<app>/storage`, which on a
# container platform is destroyed on every redeploy — the database rows survive
# and point at files that no longer exist.
# Read in: src/lib/operations/storage.ts, src/lib/operations/storageConfig.ts
#
# Set this to a persistent volume mounted OUTSIDE the application directory,
# mount it at the same path on every instance, and include it in backups: it
# holds the only copy of every uploaded file. In production the app warns at
# startup if this resolves inside the application directory.
# STORAGE_ROOT=/var/lib/school-os/storage

# How many trailing X-Forwarded-For entries come from infrastructure you
# control. Used to identify the real client IP for login rate limiting.
# Read in: src/lib/auth/clientIp.ts
#
# Entries are counted from the RIGHT, because a client can forge the left-hand
# ones. Set it to the number of proxies between the internet and this app:
#   0 / unset  trust nothing (safe default; per-IP limits are then disabled)
#   1          exactly one reverse proxy or load balancer  <- most deployments
#   2          e.g. a CDN in front of a load balancer
#
# Setting this HIGHER than the real number lets an attacker forge the client IP
# and evade per-IP limits. An invalid value falls back to 0.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1

# Extra origins allowed to invoke Next Server Actions, as bare hosts.
# Read in: src/lib/config/origins.mjs (via next.config.mjs)
#
# Usually unnecessary: Next only consults this list when a request's Origin
# differs from its Host, so a normal same-origin deployment needs nothing here.
# Set it when a reverse proxy does not forward the public hostname, or when the
# app is served on more than one hostname.
#
# APP_ORIGINS takes precedence over APP_ORIGIN; both accept a full URL and are
# reduced to a host. Read at BUILD time, so it must be set for `npm run build`.
# APP_ORIGIN=school.edu.et
# APP_ORIGINS=school.edu.et,portal.school.edu.et


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. OPTIONAL TUNING
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Maximum PostgreSQL connections in the pool. Defaults to 10.
# Read in: src/db/client.ts
# Keep the total across all instances below the server's max_connections.
# PG_POOL_MAX=10


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. DEVELOPMENT ONLY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Directory for the embedded PGlite database used when NODE_ENV is not
# production. Defaults to `.data/pgdata`. Ignored entirely in production.
# Read in: src/db/config.ts
# PGLITE_DATA_DIR=.data/pgdata


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. NOT SET HERE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# NEXT_RUNTIME   Set by Next.js itself, not by you. src/instrumentation.ts
#                reads it to run startup checks only on the Node.js runtime.
#
# __SOS_ORIGIN_NOTICE
#                Internal. next.config.mjs sets it on itself so the APP_ORIGIN
#                notice is printed once rather than once per build worker.
#                Do not set it; doing so only suppresses that notice.
#
# SMS / payment provider credentials
#                None are read from the environment today, because no provider
#                is connected: src/lib/sms/provider.ts sends nothing and
#                messages are stored with status `unconfigured` rather than
#                being falsely reported as sent. Per-school SMS settings live
#                in the database, and `apiKeyRef` there NAMES a credential
#                rather than holding one. When a real provider is added, its
#                variable belongs in this file and in the README.
#
# Session/encryption secret
#                Not used. Session tokens are 256-bit random values stored as
#                SHA-256 hashes in the database (src/lib/auth/session.ts);
#                there is no signing key to configure or rotate.
